        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "In the Ottoman Empire, the rich and poor could enjoy the services of the local hammam, a public bath that was often one component of a külliye. Visitors would undress to a modest minimum and then proceed through a sequence of bathing rooms, from cold to hot. Ottoman baths were important to both the religious and social life of the neighborhoods, providing opportunity for ritual bathing and gendered space to speak freely. \nIn practice and design, the hammam evolved from a combination of the old Greek and Roman baths of the Byzantine period along with the Islamic baths common during the Umayyad Caliphate. The Ottomans continued these practices and imbued them with a style all their own as can be seen in the luxurious, domed hot rooms of the traditional hammams still operating in Istanbul."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "uniques": [
            "[40]% Food is carried over after population increases [in this city]",
            "[+1 Culture, +2 Gold] from every specialist [in this city]"
        ],
        "requiredTech": "Engineering"
    },
    {
        "name": "Külliye",
        "replaces": "Museum",
        "uniqueTo": "Devlet-i ʿAlīye-i ʿOsmānīye",
        "culture": 5,
        "production": 4,
        "cost": 225,
        "specialistSlots": { "Artist": 2 },
        "requiredBuilding": "Opera House",
        "maintenance": 3,
        "hurryCostModifier": 0,
        "requiredTech": "Architecture",
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Centered around a mosque, the külliye was a complex of buildings dedicated to the spiritual, physical, and mental wellbeing of the community. They could be found peppered throughout Ottoman cities, locating hospitals, schools, and kitchens around the central place of worship. \nIn Istanbul, the many külliye were built to sculpt the landscape, marking it as owned by the Sultan. The most stunning example is the Süleymaniye Külliye, named for its sponsor Süleyman the Magnificent. Together with the central mosque, it had seven schools, a hostel, hospitals, and shops, similar to the diversity of buildings and services we would find on a modern university campus. Today, külliye can still be found throughout Anatolia. Many have been renovated into museums; others are still part of their communities, the core of a neighborhood or suburb."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "uniques": [
            "Destroyed when the city is captured",
            "Comment [Unlocked earlier at [Architecture] vs [Archaeology]]"
        ]
    },

    {
        "name": "Bāq",
        "replaces": "Garden",
        "uniqueTo": "Qajar Iran",
        "culture": 2,
        "happiness": 1,
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The bāq, or garden, is an evolution of the Achaemenid pairidaeza. It was designed as a paradise on earth, with geometrical arrangements, architectural elements, and carefully selected arrays of plants. Bāqs may be private or public, ranging from palace gardens to city parks. In all forms, they offer respite and contemplation, and, within cities, a breath of fresh air."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "uniques": ["[+25]% Great Person generation [in this city]"],
        "hurryCostModifier": 25,
        "maintenance": 1,
        "requiredTech": "Theology"
    },

    {
        "name": "Huiguan",
        "replaces": "Bank",
        "uniqueTo": "Qing",
        "cost": 200,
        "hurryCostModifier": 160,
        "gold": 2,
        "science": 2,
        "culture": 2,
        "percentStatBonus": { "gold": 25 },
        "specialistSlots": { "Merchant": 1 },
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "One challenge for the center of any empire is handling the multiplicity and diversity of its people. Imperial China contained a vast array of people from different provinces who spoke different languages, all with their own networks and links across the empire. These informal networks were formalized within the institutions of the huiguan, the guild hall. When established in distant cities, these places were simultaneously mercantile assemblages, social gathering spots, lending banks, legal resources, and places for the collective worship of particular deities or ancestors. \nBeijing in the Qing era held hundreds of huiguan, tied to various provinces across the empire: prominent examples include the Sichuan Huiguan, the Wuhu Huiguan, and the Hunan and Hubei Huiguan. Today, the management of migrant communities in urban China has transitioned to the state via the hukou system (registration for permanent residence) and is attached to a legal set of rights over where one can access services."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "requiredTech": "Banking"
    },

